{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"genius-titanium-husky","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Symptom\n\nClosing a ticket aborted with an error that is **both a hard failure and a misdiagnosis**:\n\n    $ rebar transition c97f-90ae-4976-47c7 in_progress closed\n    Error: cannot close c97f-90ae-4976-47c7: completion verification could not run\n      (step 'decide@then/verify' failed: completion recovery context bound exceeded).\n      The completion-verification gate is enabled\n      (verify.require_completion_verification_for_close); install the 'agents' extra\n      and set a model API key, or override with --force-close=\"<reason>\".\n\nThe remedy it suggests is wrong. The `agents` extra **was** installed and a model API key **was**\npresent — proven immediately: re-running the identical command with no environment change produced a\nnormal verdict (`completion verification FAILED ... 1 unmet criteria`), i.e. the verifier ran fine.\nAn operator following the message would go reinstall dependencies to fix a problem that is actually\n\"this ticket's text is too long\".\n\n## Root cause\n\n`src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_recovery.py:145-152` raises `CompletionRecoveryError` when the\nassembled context exceeds `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS`, defined at line 41:\n\n    _MAX_CRITERION_CHARS       = 4_000\n    _MAX_TOTAL_CRITERIA_CHARS  = 32_000\n    _MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS         = 24_000\n\nMeasured on the ticket that triggered it (`c97f-90ae-4976-47c7`):\n\n| material | chars |\n|---|---|\n| description alone | **25,516** |\n| 9 comments | 22,458 |\n| combined | 47,974 |\n\n**The description ALONE (25,516) exceeds the 24,000 context bound.** So for this ticket the recovery\npath can never succeed — any close that falls back to recovery hard-errors, permanently.\n\nThat the retry then worked is not luck about the bound: it means the PRIMARY verification path\nsucceeded on the second attempt and recovery was never entered. Recovery is a fallback, so the bug\nonly surfaces when the primary path stumbles — which makes it look intermittent while actually being\ndeterministic for any oversized ticket.\n\n## Why the bound is reachable by legitimate work, not abuse\n\n25.5k of description is not a pathological ticket. It is what a plan survives after being hardened\nthrough repeated `review-plan` cycles: this one went through six passes, each of which *added*\nrequired specificity (decisions recorded, alternatives rejected with reasons, false premises\ncorrected in place, acceptance criteria made precisely checkable). The gates reward exactly the\nverbosity that then breaks the close gate. Two rebar subsystems are pulling in opposite directions.\n\n## Impact\n\n1. **A ticket can become uncloseable through normal use.** The only offered escape is\n   `--force-close`, which bypasses completion verification entirely — trading a precise gate for no\n   gate, on a ticket whose plan was thorough enough to trip the bound.\n2. **The error misattributes the cause**, sending operators to a dependency/credential remedy that\n   cannot help. The diagnostic already carries the real numbers (`context_chars`,\n   `context_char_limit`) but they are not surfaced in the message.\n3. **It presents as flaky.** Identical command, no environment change, different failure mode.\n\n## Suggested direction (not prescriptive)\n\n- Surface the real cause: report `context_chars` vs `context_char_limit` and name the oversized\n  material, instead of the generic install-the-extra text. This alone removes the misdirection.\n- Decide whether the close gate's recovery path should *degrade* rather than abort — e.g. verify\n  criteria in batches, or truncate context deterministically with the omission recorded in the\n  verdict — so a long-but-legitimate plan stays closeable without `--force-close`.\n- Reconsider the relationship between the bounds: `_MAX_TOTAL_CRITERIA_CHARS` is 32,000 while\n  `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS` is 24,000, so a ticket can hold acceptance criteria that are individually and\n  collectively legal yet assemble into an over-bound context.\n\n## Circumstances (how it was found)\n\nHit while closing story J5 [rebar:c97f-90ae-4976-47c7] of epic e369 during a `/rebar-implement` run,\nimmediately after its acceptance evidence went green (GitHub Actions run 30512622682: harness built,\nbooted, 4/4 smoke tests passed, clean teardown). The close was blocked not by the evidence but by\nthe verifier's own context handling.\n\nThe ticket was large **because the process made it so**: I had recorded operational findings on it as\ncomments during execution (three failed local harness runs, the two verified-ineffective AMPS\nproperty names, the registry evidence that the planned image does not exist), exactly as the\nwork-tracking guidance asks. Those comments are 22,458 of the 47,974 chars. So following the\n\"record your work in rebar\" discipline is itself a way to reach this failure.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] the error message for a bound breach reports the measured size and the limit, and does NOT\n      suggest installing the `agents` extra or setting an API key when both are demonstrably present\n- [ ] a ticket whose description alone exceeds `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS` can still complete a\n      completion-verification close without `--force-close`, OR the failure explains precisely which\n      material to shorten\n- [ ] a regression test builds a ticket with a >24,000-char description and asserts the close path\n      neither raises the misleading dependency error nor aborts uncloseably\n- [ ] the interaction between `_MAX_TOTAL_CRITERIA_CHARS` (32,000) and `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS` (24,000)\n      is documented or reconciled, so criteria that pass their own bound cannot assemble into a\n      context that fails","id":"d59e-e042-2b94-4b70","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Completion-verification close aborts on a context bound and blames a missing agents extra"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785385395501082001,"uuid":"e7c5f50a-0e3e-45e2-87df-be757c6a3893"}